  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Updating Package.json Scripts**\n\nI\'m currently modifying the `/package.json` scripts. My focus is on ensuring they properly support a full-stack Express + Vite server. I\'m updating the `\"dev\"` script to use `tsx server.ts`, as Node 22 natively supports TypeScript execution, and the `\"start\"` script to `node server.ts`.\n\n\n**Designing Shared Types and Backend**\n\nI\'ve moved on to creating the foundational elements of the application. I\'m carefully designing the shared types in `src/types.ts`, focusing on clear, detailed structures for data retrieved from GitHub. I\'m also starting work on the Express backend (`server.ts`), and want to integrate both as seamlessly as possible. The type definitions are designed to capture important metadata for repositories, commits, pull requests, releases, contributors, and velocity points.\n\n\n**Defining Data Structures Further**\n\nI\'m now refining the data structures I\'ll be using. I\'ve broken down the translation objects for commits, pull requests, and releases. I\'m focusing on attributes like `emoji`, `plainTitle`, `whyItMatters`, and `category`. I\'m also planning to build an `AiDigest` object, which will include a headline, playful summary, momentum mood and emoji, and an estimated next milestone with grounded progress.\n\n\n**Expanding Data Structures**\n\nI\'m expanding the data structures. I\'ve defined new interfaces: `ChatMessage`, `MilestoneAlert`, and `IslandStation`. These are crucial for handling chat interactions, displaying milestone updates, and visualizing data in a spatial environment. I\'ve also incorporated the necessary properties for each.\n\nI\'m also focusing on the `server.ts` file, ensuring it\'s robust and ready to go. I\'m adding `dotenv/config` at the top and initialize Google GenAI using the `@google/genai` library, with a custom User-Agent.\n\n\n**Implementing GitHub Interaction**\n\nI\'m now implementing the interaction with GitHub. I\'m leveraging the Google GenAI library, specifying the `gemini-4-hydrogen` model. I\'ve built a helper function, `parseRepoInput`, which handles various GitHub URL formats, including those with branches and whitespace. I\'m also implementing an ETag and short-TTL memory cache to minimize GitHub API calls and improve performance significantly. This caching strategy checks for freshness, and uses `If-None-Match` for efficient updates. I\'m fetching repository metadata and commits in parallel for speed.\n\n\n**Analyzing GitHub Data**\n\nI\'m now focusing on specific GitHub API calls to gather the necessary data. I\'m prioritizing the last 15 commits, and top 10 merged pull requests, and the latest 5 releases. I\'m building a velocity series by aggregating timestamps for commits and merged pull requests over the past 10 active days, using data for the last 14 calendar days, with a `per_page=30` commits fetch. I\'m handling 404 and 403/429 errors gracefully, and displaying the latest 12\342\200\22315 commits in the feed!\nI am also building the POST /api/ai/summarize endpoint and designing a grounded prompt using the repo\'s metadata and commits.\n\n\n**Integrating AI Models**\n\nI\'m now integrating the AI models for summarization and chat. I\'ve implemented the `/api/ai/summarize` endpoint, which calls `ai.models.generateContent` to produce an `AiDigest` JSON object, handling potential Gemini errors gracefully by returning appropriate HTTP status codes and messages. For the `/api/ai/chat` endpoint, I\'m streaming responses via Server-Sent Events, using a rich system instruction to ground the assistant in live repository data and guide it to explain technical concepts with analogies for non-developers.\n\n\n"
    message_id: "QcQCa7DnMf21odAP2YqoWA"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_5696893297044300284"
