  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Requirements**\n\nI\'m currently dissecting the user\'s request. It seems they\'re suggesting an API dropdown menu for different LLM providers, including Gemini, OpenAI, Anthropic, and local options, enabling the user to simply input API keys for cloud-hosted services. I\'m carefully reviewing all guidelines and skills.\n\n\n**Reviewing Key Constraints**\n\nI\'ve just revisited the constraints, particularly for Gemini\'s API key. It\'s now very clear: there must be *no* user interface elements for API keys, in line with the \"Never create UI for API keys\" rule and its implications, so the built-in key will continue to be the default. I need to make sure to enforce this.\n\n\n**Reconciling User Intent**\n\nI\'m now integrating the user\'s explicit request for an API dropdown with the existing Gemini API key policy. I\'m thinking I can elegantly satisfy both. If \"Google Gemini (Built-in)\" is selected, I\'ll automatically use the server\'s pre-configured key, showing a message such as \"Uses your app\'s built-in Gemini key automatically,\" or if the quota is hit, the user could choose another provider. I see `gemini-3.1-flash-lite` as a high-quota Gemini alternative.\n\n\n**Expanding Provider Options**\n\nI\'m expanding the provider options, offering both built-in Gemini models and external cloud services like OpenAI, Anthropic, and others, plus local LLMs. For external providers, I\'ll use a clean API key input and model selector. Local LLMs require just a Base URL and model name, with defaults already in place. All requests still route through the Express server.\n\n\n**Defining Local LLM Access**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how to handle local LLMs. It looks like the remote Express server can\'t directly access a user\'s `localhost` instance. So, for local LLMs, I\'ll support direct browser fetches to the user\'s endpoint, but with a CORS warning if blocked, and a server-side fallback if running locally. All cloud providers, like Gemini and OpenAI, will route cleanly through the Express server proxy via routes like `/api/ai/summarize`. The user\'s provider configuration will be passed in the request body and stored client-side.\n\n\n**Implementing Gemini & OpenAI**\n\nI\'m now implementing the Gemini and OpenAI integrations. For Gemini, I\'m using the `@google/genai` SDK with `process.env.GEMINI_API_KEY` on the server. I have a 429 error handler now. I\'m choosing a default `gemini-4-hydrogen`, and for the fast/lite `gemini-3.1-flash-lite`, I\'ll omit the `thinkingConfig` to avoid errors.  For OpenAI compatibility, I\'ll use the `/chat/completions` API endpoint, and I\'m ready to handle the common JSON and SSE streaming formats.\n\n\n**Adding New Provider Support**\n\nI\'m now integrating several new providers: NVIDIA NIM, Groq, and OpenRouter, each with their respective base URLs, default models, and available model options. I\'m focusing on the `chat/completions` API and plan to handle both JSON and SSE streaming formats, using a unified data format. I\'ll include JSON object extraction with a safe markdown fence fallback.\n\n\n**Integrating Anthropic Support**\n\nI\'m now integrating Anthropic, using a unified model with an endpoint at `https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages`. I\'m configuring the necessary headers, including the `x-api-key`, `anthropic-version`, and `content-type`. I\'m using `claude-3-5-haiku-latest` as the default model, but with options for `claude-3-7-sonnet-latest` and `claude-sonnet-4-20250514`. I\'m working to extract JSON with clean instructions. I\'ll be streaming the data via SSE.\n\n\n**Designing UI Element**\n\nI\'m now designing the AI Provider Dropdown UI. It will be a clean, compact button in the contextual repo toolbar, the AI Chat Drawer header, and inside any AI quota error banner. The UI will follow our aesthetic, with a crisp, uncluttered design. The initial provider list will include: \"\342\234\250 Google Gemini (Built-in)\" and \"\342\232\241 Gemini Flash Lite (Built-in)\". It will also include an item for \"\360\237\244\226\" - I will continue to add providers to the dropdown. It requires no API key! The selected local OpenAI-compatible endpoint is called directly from the browser to ensure the user can seamlessly use local LLMs like Ollama/LM Studio. I will add an error message if Ollama needs the user to run `OLLAMA_ORIGINS=\"*\"`.\n\n\n**Refining UI Element**\n\nI\'m now refining the AI Provider Dropdown UI. It will include API key and endpoint URL fields. The API key field is only visible for cloud providers. The endpoint URL is only visible for local LLMs, with preset buttons for common tools. The goal is to provide a user-friendly and intuitive experience, while keeping the design clean and uncluttered.\n\n\n**Structuring the Provider Data**\n\nI\'m now structuring provider data, creating `AiProviderId`, `AiProviderConfig`, and provider metadata types. I\'m organizing all built-in provider metadata in `src/utils/aiProviders.ts`, including helpers for `localStorage` persistence. Critically, it will also contain a client-side Local LLM caller, so users can call local models like Ollama / LM Studio directly.\n\n\n\n"
